pipeline {
agent none
stages {
stage('Build') {
agent { label 'linux && trusted-build' }
options { timeout(time: 20, unit: 'MINUTES') }
steps { sh './ci/build.sh' }
}

stage('Untrusted PR Tests') {
when { changeRequest() }
agent { label 'linux && isolated-pr' }
steps { sh './ci/test-pr.sh' }
}
stage('Release') {
when { branch 'main' }
agent { label 'linux && release-signer' }
steps { sh './ci/release.sh' }
}
}
}
Architecture and Operating Decisions

Set controller executors to zero and place administrative tasks on a dedicated restricted agent if needed.

Use ephemeral agents for general CI where startup and cost permit; use dedicated agents only for justified
hardware, licensing, locality, or security needs.

Separate agent pools by trust and network zone, not only by operating system.

Limit executor count based on memory, disk, container runtime, ports, licenses, and workload interference.

Pin agent images and automatically expire outdated images or nodes after a controlled transition period. Production Checklist

Each agent pool has a defined trust tier, owner, image, labels, network path, identity, and cleanup behavior.

Executor counts are validated with load tests and resource monitoring.

Untrusted workloads cannot reuse trusted workspaces, caches, credentials, or networks.

Agent images and toolchains are versioned and patchable.

Queue, provisioning, utilization, failure, and cleanup metrics exist per label or pool.

