parameters {
booleanParam(name: 'RUN_INTEGRATION', defaultValue: true)
}
stages {
stage('Build and Unit Test') {
agent { label 'linux && trusted-build' }
steps {
sh 'set -euo pipefail; ./ci/build-and-test.sh'
archiveArtifacts artifacts: 'dist/**', fingerprint: true
junit testResults: 'reports/unit/*.xml', allowEmptyResults: false
}
}
stage('Integration') {
when { expression { params.RUN_INTEGRATION } }
agent { label 'linux && integration' }
steps { retry(2) { sh './ci/integration.sh' } }
}
}
post {
always { archiveArtifacts artifacts: 'reports/**', allowEmptyArchive: true }
cleanup { deleteDir() }
}
}
Architecture and Operating Decisions

Use Declarative Pipeline as the default and require a documented reason for large Scripted sections.

Keep Jenkinsfile orchestration thin; place build, test, scan, and deploy implementation in versioned scripts
or shared libraries.

Apply global and stage timeouts, bounded retries, concurrency rules, retention, and cleanup intentionally.

Publish test results, artifact identity, provenance, logs, and deployment evidence even when later stages fail.

Design every mutating stage to be idempotent, resumable, or protected by a recorded checkpoint and
explicit recovery path. Production Checklist

The Jenkinsfile is committed with the application and reviewed through the normal change process.

Every stage has a purpose, agent requirement, timeout, input, output, and failure behavior.

Substantial logic is testable outside Jenkins.

Credentials are injected only around the smallest required step and are not interpolated unsafely.

Artifacts and evidence remain available after partial failure and controller restart.

